A||N||14||Monkeyman||Computer question----sound board installation||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||12-02-2000||04:57 PM||||I have a year old Gateway computer with 70k miles on it (wait...wrong mode of transportation).  I just bought a Sound Blaster Live! Value Edition sound card.  How do I install it???  It came with the sound card and a couple of installation CDs that I have to assume is nothing more than the drivers.  Do I just pull the old card out and replace it with the new one??  Seems too easy.  I would imagine that the original card is "built in" since the output jack is part of the tower instead of being inserted into one of the "spare" slots (I have no idea what the technical terms for this stuff is).  This new card looks like it fits into one of the empty "slots".<P>Suggestions (or rather detailed installation procedures) would be greatly welcome.  I'd like to get this installed so I can start converting my cassettes to CDs. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||vietnow||12-02-2000||05:45 PM||||if your old sound card isnt actually a "card" (which means it plugs into one of those slots) then you dont have to worry about taking it out.<P><BR>Installing things on computers is pretty idiot proofed these days (not calling you an idiot) <P>all you should have to do is plug it in. but make sure the power cord is unplugged before you do anything.<P><BR>so, just plug it in, turn it on, it should find it, and ask for the cd or it might even already have drivers for it.. but you will want to install the drivers off the cd since you know they are the right ones.<P>once you have it installed, and working, you might want to look for new drivers on the web.<P>if you have any trouble just ask<P><P>------------------<BR>Vietnow --<BR>1986 Celica GTS||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-02-2000||06:07 PM||||Sounds easy enough.  (Yes.  I am an idiot when it comes to these things.)  Does this mean that the original sound "card" will be non functional?  (Not a problem, just don't want to plug something into it and fry something.)<P>I'll have to try that in the morning.  Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||12-03-2000||01:42 AM||||If the soundcard is built into the motherboard, you should be able to disable it in CMOS (usually hit DEL on startup). However, it still might just be a card, you never know. But they have been appearing on motherboards quite frequently these days.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-03-2000||03:10 AM||||Johnny is correct about some mobos have it built on the board and you have to disable it in the bios. Either way, I suggest removing the old drivers first. Go to the my computer icon, then control panel, then system, then device manager, find the audio driver(s), highlight it and the click on the remove button.<P>Now install the board. Be careful about working inside the case. Like was said, unplug the power cord first. Touch as little as possible inside, but always first touch the metal chassis to get rid of any static (which is very, very bad for computer components). You might have to remove one of the slot covers on the back.<P>Is your new card use a PCI or an ISA slot? Hope it's a PCI, they're generally better and the ISA slots on motherboards are being phased out. Once it's installed, power back up. It should be plug and play. At some point it will ask for the install disk. Hope it works for you.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-03-2000||09:42 AM||||I got it installed last night.  I have no idea what the difference is between a PCI and an ISA slot, though.  All of the places to plug in look the same (only had 2 left--total of 4--one is for my modem and the other is a SCSI something or other having to do with my scanner).  I just plugged it in to the one I came to first.  Installation disc in, loaded 'er up (seemed to take forever) and now she seems to work.  I wish I had gotten some sort of instruction manual, though.  I'm just clicking on things trying to find out what does what.  Very annoying.  Anyone else have the same problem with either Sound Blaster or Aberdeen (online co. I bought it through)?  Someone gets a nasty letter this week.<P>Should I still go in and try to disable the original sound card?  It appears to be part of the motherboard (is that what a mobo is, bb?) as there just aren't any other cards in there.  I've found some menus that give me the choice of what I've got or the original (SB Audio 64D or something like that).  I just changed those to the new card.  Is that good enough?  I don't want to completely delete the old card in case something happens to this one.<P>Does anyone know any source (website, etc) where I can download an instruction manual?<P>Enough long windedness (yes, that's a word <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) for now.  Thanx for the bandwidth.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||12-03-2000||01:26 PM||||Monkeyman, a couple of things:<P>Assuming your card is a real Sound Blaster and not a "SB compatible" you should be able to go to <A HREF="http://www.creative.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.creative.com</A>  and poke around.  It sounds like you got an OEM package which is basically just the card in an anti-static bag and a CD with drivers, sometimes with a small instruction book.  The retail version of the card would cost more but retail versions usually come with more extensive documentation. <P>As for PCI vs ISA, ISA is the old 8/16 bit card bus from the old IBM PC/XT/AT days with a maximum rated throughput of around 4Mbytes/second (depending on bus speed).  It's used mostly for old legacy devices with modest needs like internal modems and older sound cards. Most current motherboards have 1 or at most 2 ISA bus slots.<P>PCI is the 32 bit bus @133 Mbytes/second throughput. There is a 64 bit version @ 266Mbytes/second but that's not common on desktop PCs. Most current motherboards have between 4 and 6 PCI cards. PCI also has other features which make it more friendly, especially if you have lots of cards to plug in. <P>AGP is just for video cards.  It uses a different path to the processor, keeping a bunch of traffic off the PCI bus. Depending on the speed (1X, 2X, 4X) the AGP bus speed can be up to 533Mbytes/second. <P>There is a web site <A HREF="http://www.resellerratings.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.resellerratings.com</A>  where buyers rate online computer hardware vendors.  It's not perfect but it can give you some idea of what you might be getting yourself into. Abderdeen isn't particularly well rated. <P>Disabling the onboard sound card is usually done via the bios or a jumper on the motherboard. You're better of disabling it if you can.  If you're running a M$ OS you can disable or remove the device by going into Settings -&gt; System -&gt; Hardware -&gt; Device Manager, clicking on "Sound, video, and game controllers" and selecting the actual device, it will be something like "Creative SoundBlaster".  If you delete in software without disabling the hardware it will in all probablility be automatically be detected (thank you PnP) when you reboot. <BR>If you need to go back to the old card, just reenable it in the bios/jumper, reboot, and redetect the hardware. <P>This is pretty long-winded too but I hope it helps at least a bit.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-03-2000||01:40 PM||||Thanx, guys.  I think (not 100% sure <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) I have it figured out now.  I disabled/removed the old/stock/built in sound card.  I have a real SB SoundBlaster Live! Value card but it <I>is</I> an OEM version.  I wondered what that meant when I bought it.  I'll try to get on their site tomorrow and see if they have any documentation that I can download.  If not, I'll just continue to play with the controls until I can figure everything out on my own.<P>BTW--The slots must be PCI slots.  My computer is only about a year old and I do have 4 of them (all identical as near as I can tell).<P>If anyone has the same sound card (love it BTW!) and has the original documentation they'd like to get rid of, please email me privately regarding a gift/sale/scan/etc.<P>Thanx.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-03-2000||03:39 PM||||MM, yes you got what's called an OEM card...which is no fancy packaging or instructions, etc. The advantage is that it's cheaper, but they assume you know what you're doing. The other option was to spend more and get the retail version. Regardless, there should be a readme.txt file or something similar on the CD. If not, check out the Creative Labs website, often you can download a PDF file containing the instructions. Yep, sounds like you have all PCI slots in yo mommaboard (mobo).||||reg||1
